1 COMMAND
2 PARAMETERS
    The descriptions of the commands include various parameter types.  These
    are denoted by a name in angle brackets.  For example, <address> means
    that the parameter is to be an address value.

    This help entry describes the command parameter types shown in the other
    help entries.

    <address>           This must be a valid address value.  If virtual memory
                        is enabled in the VAX, then it is a virtual address
                        and will be translated accordingly.  If virtual memory
                        is not enabled, it is interpreted as a physical
                        address.

                        It may be a numeric value, either in the default
                        radix (see the SET RADIX command) or with an explicit
                        radix specifier prefix like D^ for decimal or X^ for
                        hexadecimal.

                        It can also be a symbolic value, which is a name
                        optionally enclosed in single quotes.  The symbolic
                        value is replaced with it's numeric value and then
                        used.

    <value>             A numeric value.  By default this is a 32-bit value,
                        since that is the natural data size for VAX.  It can
                        be in the default radix (see the SET RADIX command)
                        or can have an explicity radix specifier prefix of
                        D^ for a decimal value or X^ for a hexadecimal value.

    Note that in the case of both <value> and <address> you can construct
    an integer expression to represent the value.  You can use +, -, *, and
    / operators, and parenthesis.  Note that an expression that contains a
    symbol can only use symbols that are already defined.  (An assembler
    program can use forward references; see ASM EXPRESSIONS.)
